12:07.118 --> 12:08.451
that aren't completely idiotic.

12:08.453 --> 12:10.061
Look, I can't do this right now.

12:10.063 --> 12:12.046
    We're pretty into our book
      about Chairman Mayo.

12:12.123 --> 12:13.898
          -(meows): Mao.
    -(chuckles): Sorry, sorry.

12:13.975 --> 12:16.526
    Get out of here, Brian,
     you're messing me up.

12:19.314 --> 12:22.315
    Meg, I need to talk to you
          about Pouncey.

12:22.391 --> 12:24.242
         It is my duty
      as this family's dog

12:24.319 --> 12:26.152
         to protect it
      when I sense danger.

12:26.229 --> 12:28.154
And I sense danger with Pouncey.

12:28.231 --> 12:30.156
Look, you may be our family dog,

12:30.233 --> 12:32.250
    but you've never really
           been my dog.

12:32.327 --> 12:34.978
        I'm a punching bag
     at school and at home.

12:34.980 --> 12:37.981
 And there were some days where
 I could have really used a dog

12:37.983 --> 12:38.982
      to help cheer me up.

12:38.984 --> 12:40.241
       And where were you?

12:40.318 --> 12:42.502
     Hanging out with Dad
     or-or Chris or Stewie

12:42.578 --> 12:44.837
        for your little
    time travel adventures.

12:44.839 --> 12:46.155
You know about the time machine?

12:46.157 --> 12:47.915
Yeah, my room's right next door.

12:47.993 --> 12:49.917
 That thing's loud as (bleep).

12:49.919 --> 12:50.935
     I have a new pet now.

12:50.937 --> 12:53.438
    One that's there for me
        when I need her.

12:53.515 --> 12:54.998
          Unlike you.

12:55.000 --> 12:56.849
          Okay. I see.

12:56.926 --> 12:58.851
       (microwave chimes)

12:58.853 --> 13:01.004
      You have a microwave
         in your room?

13:01.006 --> 13:03.172
        Yeah, I got tired
        of people groaning

13:03.174 --> 13:04.598
       every time I went
       into the kitchen.

13:04.676 --> 13:07.860
        Oh. You got, uh,
    Redenbacher's up in there?

13:07.862 --> 13:09.120
             -Yeah.
           -Oh, word?

13:09.197 --> 13:11.364
        Brian, stop trying
        to sound young.

13:11.366 --> 13:12.957
           (chuckles)
         Okay, Booger.

13:13.034 --> 13:15.376
       -(scoffs) Boomer.
             -Boomer.

13:19.949 --> 13:22.116
           (purring)

13:22.193 --> 13:24.385
 You make me so happy, Pouncey.

13:26.214 --> 13:30.199
    This is gonna sound weird,
    but do you want to dance?

13:30.201 --> 13:33.144
   ("Good Man in a Bad Time"
     by Ian Hunter playing)

13:39.544 --> 13:41.694
       This is fun, right?

14:04.402 --> 14:07.262
    Whoa, did this just get
           super hot?

14:14.913 --> 14:18.147
     You're mine now, Meg.

14:20.193 --> 14:21.842
              Ow.

14:21.920 --> 14:23.736
          I like "ow."

14:24.847 --> 14:26.680
       Hey. Whoa, weird.

14:26.758 --> 14:29.284
       Sorry to interrupt.
  I'm just cleaning my cleats.

14:33.874 --> 14:36.949
            (gasps)
    Cats, kittens and cats.

14:37.026 --> 14:39.360
Cat lady. Lots of plastic bags.

14:39.437 --> 14:41.954
   Tissues in-in my sleeves.
        Velcro sneakers.

14:42.031 --> 14:43.940
       Funny dish towels.

14:43.942 --> 14:46.292
        (gasps, panting)

14:46.294 --> 14:47.960
             Good.

14:48.037 --> 14:51.264
  Come, Meg. It's time to go.

14:55.803 --> 14:57.729
       Say goodbye to Meg.

14:59.974 --> 15:02.317
     -(door opens, closes)
          -Who's Ned?

15:08.074 --> 15:10.742
Knock-knock? Meg, you in there?

15:10.744 --> 15:14.637
Look, I just wanted to apologize
       for the other day.

15:14.639 --> 15:16.331
             Hello?

15:23.314 --> 15:25.423
  What the hell does this say?

15:40.106 --> 15:43.757
          -Oh, my God.
-QUAGMIRE: She left, didn't she?

15:43.835 --> 15:47.261
           -Quagmire?
  -I heard everything you were
  saying about Pouncey, Brian,

15:47.338 --> 15:49.172
        and you were right
        to be concerned.

15:49.174 --> 15:51.616
  I knew it. Wait, how did you
    hear everything I said?

15:51.618 --> 15:55.194
I put a cat-cam in your house.
It was a repurposed shower-cam.

15:55.271 --> 15:57.180
    It's standard cat adoption
           procedure.

15:57.182 --> 15:59.123
        -Is it? Really?
           -Yep. Yep.

15:59.200 --> 16:00.533
    -The agency knew about it?
             -Yep.

16:00.535 --> 16:02.276
-Because it seems weird that...
     -Let it go, you prude.

16:02.278 --> 16:03.944
  We're losing valuable time.

16:04.022 --> 16:06.280
     Sometimes cats do have
       ulterior motives.

16:06.282 --> 16:08.115
   Sometimes, when they find
       the right person,

16:08.193 --> 16:09.783
        they enslave them
       to do their bidding

16:09.861 --> 16:11.360
  for the rest of their lives.

16:11.379 --> 16:14.806
Someone to take care of all the
 feral cats in a neighborhood.

16:14.808 --> 16:16.048
       A crazy cat lady?

16:16.125 --> 16:17.883
          -That's right.
     -How do they do that?

16:17.960 --> 16:19.978
   Their poop. Toxoplasmosis.

16:20.054 --> 16:21.962
 It can make people act crazy.

16:22.040 --> 16:24.148
       It's a real thing
    discovered by this guy.

16:26.561 --> 16:28.302
         Oh, dear God.

16:28.379 --> 16:30.062
     So what you're saying
is that everyone who likes cats

16:30.139 --> 16:31.397
        only likes them
     because they literally

16:31.474 --> 16:33.140
   have crap in their brain?

16:33.218 --> 16:34.993
        -That's correct.
   -And people who like dogs

16:35.069 --> 16:37.144
         just like 'em
 because they're chill as hell?

16:37.222 --> 16:39.998
        -That's correct.
 -Wow, what a bulletproof fact.

16:40.074 --> 16:41.482
There's still time to save Meg.

16:41.559 --> 16:44.151
     There's a big house on
 the loneliest block in Quahog.

16:44.153 --> 16:46.062
    You can find her there.
         But go quick.

16:46.064 --> 16:49.156
    Once she puts on the fuzzy
    sweater, it's too late.

16:49.234 --> 16:50.508
 You're not gonna come with me?

16:50.585 --> 16:52.552
           I can't.

16:53.921 --> 16:56.497
             -(gasps)
       -They have me, too.

16:56.574 --> 16:59.100
           (crying):
  Go, Brian, get out of here.

17:00.595 --> 17:03.521
               ♪

17:30.199 --> 17:32.216
       I'll be right back.

17:32.218 --> 17:35.219
    I'm gonna save our Meg.

17:35.296 --> 17:37.280
      (door opens, closes)

17:37.282 --> 17:39.432
      Who the hell is Ned?

17:42.395 --> 17:44.395
         (cats mewing)

17:46.232 --> 17:48.291
    You're doing great, Meg.

17:48.293 --> 17:50.735
       Yeah, I feel good.
       Like I belong here.

17:50.737 --> 17:54.072
  -Will I get used to the...?
        -You'll get used
        to the smell, yes.

17:55.967 --> 17:58.968
Let her go. Ooh, oh, it stinks.

17:58.970 --> 18:00.319
     It's too late, Brian.

18:00.396 --> 18:03.489
 Years of ridicule and neglect
     have brought me here.

18:03.566 --> 18:05.808
       This is my destiny.

18:05.810 --> 18:09.161
          Yes, it is.
 She needs us and we need her.

18:09.238 --> 18:11.422
   And I'm not gonna let you
        get in the way.

18:11.499 --> 18:12.924
           (hisses)

18:13.000 --> 18:15.134
        -(cats yowling)
       -(Brian grunting)

18:23.010 --> 18:25.019
        (coins clinking)

18:28.499 --> 18:30.274
          (screeches)

18:31.352 --> 18:32.318
           (grunts)

18:33.855 --> 18:36.364
          (screeching)

18:38.952 --> 18:40.159
           (shouts)

18:51.798 --> 18:53.214
          (screeches)

19:00.698 --> 19:03.432
           (grunting)

19:21.068 --> 19:25.413
  Now I'm gonna make you watch
     as you lose her forever.

19:30.895 --> 19:32.244
           Midnight.

19:32.246 --> 19:34.154
 Not a sound from the pavement.

19:34.232 --> 19:36.156
             -What?
  -It's from<i> Cats,</i> you idiot.

19:36.234 --> 19:38.176
           -Oh, word?
           -MEG: Brian.

19:38.252 --> 19:41.086
 Meg, you have your whole life
         ahead of you.

19:41.163 --> 19:43.347
       Cats are amazing.

19:43.349 --> 19:45.257
But they're also complete dicks.

19:45.259 --> 19:47.593
      And you're too young
  to give it all up for them.

19:47.711 --> 19:50.855
  You have a family and a dog
      who care about you.

19:50.932 --> 19:53.357
    I don't. I have nothing.

19:53.359 --> 19:56.736
   I should be the cat lady.
      Give me the sweater.

20:01.850 --> 20:02.942
         Quagmire, no.

20:03.018 --> 20:05.703
       It's okay, Brian.
       I'm already gone.

20:07.540 --> 20:09.932
         (cats mewing)

20:09.934 --> 20:14.528
       ♪<i> Look, a new day</i> ♪

20:14.605 --> 20:17.506
         ♪<i> Has begun.</i> ♪

20:29.896 --> 20:32.304
   Looks like you were right
        about cats, Brian.

20:32.381 --> 20:34.548
        I'm sorry I didn't
          listen to you.

20:34.550 --> 20:35.882
       You're a good dog.

20:35.960 --> 20:38.218
        No, I haven't been
    a very good dog to you.

20:38.296 --> 20:40.145
    But that's gonna change.

20:40.222 --> 20:44.283
  Starting now, I'm gonna be
  the best darn dog I can be.

20:47.563 --> 20:49.305
     Me and the peanut butter
        are heading up.

20:49.307 --> 20:50.990
   See you in seven minutes.

20:51.066 --> 20:52.232
            (sighs)

20:52.310 --> 20:54.401
        Chunky's kind of
     a weird choice, yeah?

20:54.479 --> 20:57.162
             -Yep.
 -Should I pause<i> Hawaii Five-O?</i>

20:57.239 --> 20:59.298
        Nah, just watch it
      but don't delete it.